  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "The early signs of type 2 diabetes often develop slowly and can be very mild, meaning many people do not notice them for a long time."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Common Early Warning Signs"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Frequent urination: Needing to pee more often, especially at night, happens because your kidneys work overtime to filter and get rid of excess sugar."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Increased thirst: Drinking more fluids than usual is a direct result of losing fluids through frequent urination."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Fatigue and weakness: Feeling constantly tired or sluggish occurs because your body's cells cannot properly use glucose for energy."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Blurred vision: High blood sugar can change the fluid levels and swell the lenses in your eyes, making your sight blurry."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Slow-healing cuts and sores: Cuts, bruises, and wounds take much longer to heal because high sugar levels impair blood circulation."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Frequent infections: You may get more skin, urinary tract, or yeast infections because bacteria and yeast thrive on excess sugar."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Increased hunger: Feeling hungry soon after eating happens when your cells do not get enough glucose due to insulin resistance."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Tingling or numbness: A prickly feeling, pain, or numbness in your hands or feet can happen due to nerve irritation from high blood sugar."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Darkened skin patches: Velvety, dark skin patches (called acanthosis nigricans) around the neck, armpits, or groin often signal insulin resistance."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "What You Should Do Next"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Get tested: The only way to confirm type 2 diabetes is through a simple blood test (like a fasting glucose or A1C test) performed by a healthcare provider."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Learn more: Read the CDC Diabetes Symptoms Guide or visit the American Diabetes Association for more information on warning signs and early detection.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "CDC Diabetes Symptoms Guide",
                "link": "https://www.cdc.gov/diabetes/signs-symptoms/index.html"
              },
              {
                "text": "American Diabetes Association",
                "link": "https://diabetes.org/about-diabetes/warning-signs-symptoms"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Seek immediate help: See a doctor right away if you experience severe symptoms like extreme thirst, rapid breathing, confusion, or fruity-smelling breath, which can signal dangerously high blood sugar."
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
